# ${project.name}

Analyzes the dependencies of a Maven project and reports which declared dependencies are unused
and which used dependencies are not declared. The
[Maven Dependency Plugin](https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-dependency-plugin/) uses it for the
`dependency:analyze` goals.

The analysis reads the project's compiled classes and the class files of its resolved
dependencies, so run it after the `test-compile` phase, with test-scoped dependencies resolved. A missing
output directory is not an error: run too early, the analysis sees no classes and reports every
declared dependency as unused.
The library requires Java 8 and is compiled against the Maven ${mavenVersion} core APIs, which the
calling plugin's Maven runtime provides.

## Usage

Add the dependency to a Maven plugin:

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>${project.groupId}</groupId>
  <artifactId>${project.artifactId}</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

Inject `ProjectDependencyAnalyzer` and pass it the current project:

```java
@Mojo(name = "analyze", requiresDependencyResolution = ResolutionScope.TEST)
public class AnalyzeMojo extends AbstractMojo {
    @Inject
    private ProjectDependencyAnalyzer analyzer;

    @Inject
    private MavenProject project;

    @Override
    public void execute() throws MojoExecutionException {
        try {
            ProjectDependencyAnalysis analysis = analyzer.analyze(project);
            analysis.getUnusedDeclaredArtifacts().forEach(a -> getLog().warn("Unused declared: " + a));
            analysis.getUsedUndeclaredArtifacts().forEach(a -> getLog().warn("Used undeclared: " + a));
        } catch (ProjectDependencyAnalyzerException e) {
            throw new MojoExecutionException("Dependency analysis failed", e);
        }
    }
}
```

`analyze(project, excludedClasses)` takes regular expressions of class names to leave out of the
analysis, both in the project's own classes and in the dependencies.

## Results

`ProjectDependencyAnalysis`
([javadoc](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/shared/dependency/analyzer/ProjectDependencyAnalysis.html))
holds the result:

| Method | Returns |
|---|---|
| `getUsedDeclaredArtifacts()` | Declared dependencies that the bytecode uses |
| `getUsedUndeclaredArtifacts()` | Dependencies that the bytecode uses but the POM does not declare, such as transitive ones |
| `getUnusedDeclaredArtifacts()` | Declared dependencies that the bytecode does not use |
| `getTestArtifactsWithNonTestScope()` | Compile-scoped dependencies that only tests use and that no other compile or runtime dependency brings in |
| `getUsedDeclaredArtifactsWithUsages()`, `getUsedUndeclaredArtifactsWithUsages()` | The same artifacts with each used class and the project class (or `web.xml`) that uses it |
| `getUsedDependencyClasses()`, `getCandidateArtifactsByUsedClass()`, `getSelectedArtifactByUsedClass()` | Every class the bytecode references that can come from a dependency, every dependency that contains it, and the dependency the analysis credited (since 1.18.0) |
| `getAmbiguousUsedDependencyClasses()`, `getUnmappedUsedDependencyClasses()` | Referenced classes found in more than one dependency, and referenced classes found in none, leaving out project classes, excluded classes and the classes of the JDK that runs the analysis (since 1.18.0) |

`ignoreNonCompile()` drops dependencies outside the compile scope from the unused list, and
`forceDeclaredDependenciesUsage(String[])` moves the given `groupId:artifactId` entries from unused
to used. It fails when an entry is not declared or is already used, so stale entries do not linger.

## How classes are credited

- When several artifacts contain a used class, a declared artifact is credited over an undeclared
  one, and among those the first one found. Scope is not considered: a declared test-scoped artifact
  is credited even for a class that main code uses.
- A declared JAR without classes of its own, such as `junit-jupiter`, whose only class file is
  `module-info.class`, counts as used when an artifact it brings in directly is used. That artifact
  is then not reported as used undeclared.
- In a `war` project, the filter, listener and servlet classes named in `web.xml` (the war plugin's `webXml`, by default `src/main/webapp/WEB-INF/web.xml`) count as
  used.
- Inlined constants are detected: javac and ecj record the constant's declaring class in the
  constant pool, which the analyzer reads.

## Limitations

The analysis works on bytecode, not on source. It cannot see annotations with source-only
retention, links in Javadoc, or classes named only in strings, such as `Class.forName` arguments
or `META-INF/services` entries; `web.xml` is the one text file it reads. When these are the only uses of a dependency, it is reported as
unused; `forceDeclaredDependenciesUsage` is the escape hatch.

## Components

The main component is `ProjectDependencyAnalyzer`
([javadoc](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/shared/dependency/analyzer/ProjectDependencyAnalyzer.html)), which uses
`ClassAnalyzer`
([javadoc](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/shared/dependency/analyzer/ClassAnalyzer.html))
to list the classes in each artifact and `DependencyAnalyzer`
([javadoc](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/shared/dependency/analyzer/DependencyAnalyzer.html))
to collect the classes that the project's bytecode references.
